assessments:
  - discriminant: mcq
    value:
      id: 414-mcq-01
      prompt: "A small independent game studio publishes a set of original character designs on its public portfolio website. Six months later, a larger competing studio releases a mobile game that uses near-identical versions of the same characters. The independent studio never registered the designs with any IP office. Which Act protects the independent studio?"
      options:
        - The Computer Misuse Act 1990
        - The Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR
        - The Equality Act 2010
        - The Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988
      correct_index: 3
      feedback_correct: |
        Correct. Copyright under the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act
        1988 protects original artistic works (including character
        designs) from the moment of creation. Registration is not
        required for copyright to exist. Publishing the designs to a
        public portfolio does not place them in the public domain or
        grant anyone permission to copy them. The competing studio has
        infringed copyright by reproducing the works without licence.
      feedback_incorrect: |
        The answer is the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988. The
        common misconception is that copyright requires registration to
        be enforceable. It does not. Copyright arises automatically on
        creation of an original work and protects against unauthorised
        copying regardless of whether the work has been registered, paid
        for, or marked with the (c) symbol. The CMA, DPA and Equality
        Act are not engaged in this scenario.
      difficulty: foundation
  - discriminant: nei
    value:
      id: 414-nei-01
      stimulus: |
        A small online retailer commissioned a freelance illustrator to
        create a set of icons for the retailer's mobile shopping app.
        The icons were delivered and the agreed fee was paid. There was
        no written contract; the arrangement was confirmed by email and
        a verbal agreement. Six months later, the retailer began using
        the same icons on printed packaging, branded delivery vehicles
        and a paid social media advertising campaign. The illustrator,
        on seeing the new uses, contacts the retailer and demands
        either additional payment or that the new uses cease.
      question: |
        Name the legislation that governs this dispute, explain why the
        illustrator may have a stronger position than the retailer
        expects, and describe the impact on both parties.
      max_marks: 4
      mark_scheme:
        name_expected: |
          The Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988. Credit answers
          that name the Act or refer to copyright law specifically.
          Strongest answers also name section 11 (first ownership of
          copyright) and section 90 (assignment requirements).
        explain_expected: |
          Under section 11 of the Act, the first owner of copyright in
          a work is the creator, except where the creator is an employee
          producing the work in the course of employment. The
          illustrator was a freelancer, not an employee, so copyright in
          the icons sits with the illustrator from the moment of
          creation. Section 90 requires an assignment of copyright to be
          in writing and signed by the assignor; a verbal agreement plus
          payment is not enough to transfer copyright. What the retailer
          has acquired is an implied licence to use the icons for the
          purpose for which they were commissioned (here, the mobile
          shopping app). New uses on packaging, vehicles and paid
          advertising are outside the scope of that implied licence and
          amount to copyright infringement under section 16.
        impact_expected: |
          For the retailer, civil liability for copyright infringement
          under section 16. Available remedies for the illustrator
          include an injunction to stop the unauthorised uses, damages
          or an account of profits, and delivery up or destruction of
          infringing items. The retailer will probably need to
          negotiate a retrospective licence or a written assignment,
          almost certainly at a higher price than the original
          commission. For the illustrator, the leverage to obtain
          proper terms for a use they did not originally agree to. The
          wider lesson for the business is that copyright in
          commissioned creative work needs an express written
          assignment at the outset if the commissioner wants full
          control of subsequent uses.
      exemplar_answer: |
        The dispute is governed by the Copyright, Designs and Patents
        Act 1988. The illustrator's position is stronger than the
        retailer expects because of two provisions in the Act. Under
        section 11, the first owner of copyright in a work is the
        creator of that work, except where the creator is an employee
        producing the work in the course of employment. The illustrator
        was a freelancer, so copyright in the icons belongs to the
        illustrator from the moment the icons were created. Under
        section 90, an assignment of copyright must be in writing and
        signed by the assignor to be effective. A verbal agreement plus
        payment is not enough. What the retailer has therefore acquired
        is an implied licence to use the icons for the purpose for
        which they were commissioned (the mobile shopping app). The new
        uses on packaging, branded vehicles and paid advertising sit
        outside that implied licence and amount to copyright
        infringement under section 16. The impact for the retailer is
        civil liability: the illustrator can seek an injunction
        stopping the unauthorised uses, damages or an account of
        profits, and delivery up of infringing items. In practice the
        retailer will need to negotiate a retrospective licence or
        written assignment, almost certainly at a higher price than
        the original commission. The impact for the illustrator is
        leverage to set proper terms for uses that were not originally
        agreed. The wider lesson is that copyright in commissioned
        work needs an express written assignment at the outset if the
        commissioner wants control of every future use.
drill:
  - id: 414-drill-01
    cue: The UK Act that protects original literary, dramatic, musical and artistic works, plus films, sound recordings, broadcasts and published editions.
    answer: The Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 (CDPA 1988)
  - id: 414-drill-02
    cue: The legal status of a copyright work the moment it is created in fixed form, without registration, publication, or use of any symbol.
    answer: Protected automatically by copyright (no registration required)
  - id: 414-drill-03
    cue: The category under section 3 of the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 within which computer software is protected.
    answer: Literary work (software is treated as a literary work for copyright purposes)
  - id: 414-drill-04
    cue: The default duration of copyright in original literary, dramatic, musical and artistic works under UK law.
    answer: Life of the author plus 70 years
  - id: 414-drill-05
    cue: The first owner of copyright in a work created by a freelance creator on commission, in the absence of a written assignment.
    answer: The creator of the work (the freelancer), not the commissioner (section 11 of the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988)
  - id: 414-drill-06
    cue: The formality required for a valid assignment of copyright under the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988.
    answer: Written and signed by the assignor (section 90)
  - id: 414-drill-07
    cue: The UK Act that protects new inventions through a registration system administered by the UK Intellectual Property Office, with protection lasting up to 20 years.
    answer: The Patents Act 1977
  - id: 414-drill-08
    cue: The reason that a computer program as such is not normally patentable in the UK under section 1(2) of the Patents Act 1977, but a technical invention implemented in software can be.
    answer: The Act excludes programs for computers from patentability "as such", so patentability requires a technical contribution beyond the program itself
  - id: 414-drill-09
    cue: The UK Act that protects registered signs (including names, logos and certain sounds) used in trade, with protection lasting 10 years and renewable indefinitely.
    answer: The Trade Marks Act 1994
  - id: 414-drill-10
    cue: The legal status of software released under an open-source licence such as the GNU General Public Licence or the MIT licence.
    answer: Copyrighted by the author and licensed on the terms of the open-source licence; not public domain, and use that breaches the licence terms is copyright infringement
---

Six panels and a closer. IP is not one Act. The first move in any IP
scenario is to identify which regime applies. The second is to apply
the rule the regime sets.

{% panels %}

{% panel num="01_REGIMES" eyebrow="WHICH ACT APPLIES" headline="IP is [not one Act]." illo="ip-regimes" %}
- copyright :: CDPA 1988 :: original creative works, including software
- patents :: PA 1977 :: new inventions, must be registered
- trade marks :: TMA 1994 :: signs used in trade, registered for 10y
- designs :: RDA 1949 / CDPA Part III :: appearance of a product
- database right :: 1997 regs :: substantial investment in a database

For Paper 1 most scenarios sit inside copyright. Trade marks land when the scenario is about a name, logo or brand identity. Patents land only when the scenario is explicit about a registered patent.

{% takeaway %}
Name the regime first. The Name mark is the Act, not just "IP law".
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="02_AUTOMATIC" eyebrow="HOW COPYRIGHT ARISES" headline="Copyright is [automatic] on creation." illo="ip-automatic" %}
{% def %}
Copyright arises the moment an original work is **fixed in a material form**: saved to disk, drawn on paper, recorded, or otherwise captured in a way that persists beyond the moment of creation. No registration, deposit, publication or marking is required. The © symbol is informative but legally optional.
{% /def %}

Originality is a low bar. The work must originate with the author and involve some independent intellectual effort, but it does not need to be novel or artistically valuable.

{% takeaway %}
"It was not registered" is never an answer to a copyright claim. Strong answers state this explicitly.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="03_OWNERSHIP" eyebrow="FIRST-OWNERSHIP RULE" headline="The [creator] owns it first." illo="ip-ownership" %}
{% def %}
Section 11 of the CDPA 1988 sets the first-ownership rule. The first owner of copyright is the **author** of the work, with one exception: where the work is created by an **employee in the course of employment**, the first owner is the **employer**.
{% /def %}

For commissioned freelance work the freelancer is the first owner, even after payment. The commissioner gets only an implied licence to use the work for the purpose for which it was commissioned. This is the single most common IP mistake in small digital businesses and it appears regularly in Paper 1 scenarios.

{% takeaway %}
"They paid for it" does not transfer copyright. Strong answers name s11 and identify whether the creator was an employee or a freelancer.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="04_ASSIGN" eyebrow="HOW COPYRIGHT TRANSFERS" headline="Transfer needs [writing] + signature." illo="ip-assign" %}
{% def %}
Section 90 of the CDPA 1988 requires an **assignment of copyright to be in writing and signed by the assignor**. A verbal agreement plus payment is not enough. Licences can be granted on more flexible terms but only the written-and-signed assignment moves ownership.
{% /def %}

Without an assignment, what the commissioner has is an implied licence covering the original purpose only. Section 16 restricted acts (copying, issuing to the public, communicating, adapting) outside that purpose are infringement.

{% takeaway %}
Spell out s11 and s90 together. The strongest commissioned-work answers reach both sections explicitly.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="05_SOFTWARE" eyebrow="SOFTWARE-SPECIFIC" headline="Software is a [literary work]." illo="ip-software" %}
Under s3 of the CDPA 1988 computer software is protected as a **literary work**. The same automatic-on-creation rule applies. The same s11 and s90 rules apply.

{% term-card %}
s1(2)(c) PA 1977 :: programs for computers excluded "as such"
{% /term-card %}

Patents under the Patents Act 1977 protect new inventions, but s1(2)(c) excludes "programs for computers as such". A technical invention happening to be implemented in software can still be patentable, but the safe Paper 1 Name is "copyright under the CDPA 1988, not patents under the Patents Act 1977".

{% takeaway %}
For everyday software scenarios the regime is copyright. Patents only enter the picture when the question is explicit about a registered patent.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="06_OPENSOURCE" eyebrow="OPEN SOURCE" headline="Open source is [not public domain]." illo="ip-opensource" %}
{% def %}
Open-source software is **copyrighted** by its authors and **licensed** under terms that grant permission to use, modify and redistribute on specific conditions. Breaching the licence is copyright infringement under s16 just like any other unauthorised use.
{% /def %}

{% cloud %}
Permissive: {% chip %}MIT{% /chip %} {% chip %}BSD{% /chip %} {% chip %}Apache 2.0{% /chip %}
{% /cloud %}

{% cloud %}
Copyleft: {% chip %}GPL{% /chip %} {% chip %}AGPL{% /chip %} {% chip %}LGPL{% /chip %}
{% /cloud %}

Permissive licences allow use in proprietary commercial products subject to lightweight conditions (preserve the copyright notice). Copyleft licences require derivative works to be released under the same licence. Shipping closed-source commercial code that incorporates GPL software without source release is copyright infringement.

{% takeaway %}
"It is open source" is not a defence. Strong answers cite the licence as a copyright licence and name the breach.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% /panels %}

{% closer line="name the regime. [then] apply the rule." body="Working with an IP scenario in Paper 1 comes down to two moves, in this order." %}
{% step num="STEP 01" head="Name the regime" %}
Copyright (CDPA 1988) for almost every digital case involving original creative or software content. Trade marks (TMA 1994) for a name, logo or brand identity in trade. Patents (PA 1977) only when the scenario names a registered patent.
{% /step %}
{% step num="STEP 02" head="Apply the section" %}
Identify the work, the rightsholder and the infringing act. For commissioned-work scenarios reach s11 (first ownership) and s90 (written assignment) together. For open-source scenarios reach the licence terms and s16 restricted acts.
{% /step %}
{% /closer %}
